Confirmed. `rb_destroy` (`src/rbtree.c:372-390`) is the Reach — a purely structural walk:

```c
void rb_destroy(rbtree_t *t) {
    if (t == NULL) return;
    struct rb_node *cur = t->root;
    while (cur != NULL) {
        struct rb_node *next;
        if (cur->left == NULL) {
            next = cur->right;
            rb_free(cur->key);
            if (t->value_free) t->value_free(cur->value);
            rb_free(cur);
        } else {
            struct rb_node *left = cur->left;
            cur->left = left->right;
            left->right = cur;
            next = left;
        }
        cur = next;
    }
    rb_free(t);
}
```

Walking through what it touches: only `t->root`, `cur->left`, `cur->right`, `cur->key` (freed by pointer, contents never inspected), `cur->value`, and `t->value_free`. That's it.

- **`t->size`** — never read anywhere in this function. The invariant-5 corruption (`t->size += 1`) is invisible to it.
- **Key ordering/content** — `rb_free(cur->key)` frees whatever pointer is currently in that field; it never compares keys or cares which node "should" hold which string. The invariant-4 key-pointer swap just changes which node's `key` field points at which already-owned allocation — each string is still pointed to by exactly one node, so every string still gets freed exactly once.
- **`cur->color`** — never referenced anywhere in this function, not even read. The invariant-1/2/3 color flips are completely invisible to teardown.
- **`cur->parent`** — also never used by this loop (it's a right-spine rotation using only `left`/`right`), so it's irrelevant either way.

So every one of the five planned corruptions mutates only fields this function ignores, and none of them touch `left`/`right` topology or allocation state — the pointer graph `rb_destroy` walks is identical to a valid tree's. Confirmed safe.

I'll now implement one invariant at a time, starting with invariant 1 (root-red), running `make test`, `make asan`, and `make memcheck` after each before moving to the next.

